{
  "case_id": "2010-09-893",
  "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:02 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Andrew J. Pincus)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Ninth Circuit concluded in this case that a State law may mandate the use of a particular procedure in arbitration as long as the law also requires the use of that same procedure in litigation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What if -- what if a State finds it unconscionable to have an arbitration clause in an adhesion contract which requires the arbitration to be held at a great distance from -- from where the other party is and requires that party to pay the cost of the arbitration? Can a State not find that to be unconscionable?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I was under the impression, Mr. Pincus, that Discover Bank specifically cites a case which arose not in the arbitration context, but instead in the general litigation context, which is this America Online case, and thereby made clear that its rule, however different it may seem to you from normal contract provisions, its rule applied both in the arbitration sphere and in the litigation sphere."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "general unconscionability doctrine, Justice Kagan. It is a doctrine that applies only in the context of class waivers and that's the problem."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Why, why, why?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The State law at issue here is not preemptive, for three reasons. First, it is consistent with the equal footing principle or nondiscrimination principle that this Court has consistently recognized is embodied in section 2."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the savings clause in the FAA serves two critical purposes, and that is that the -- the contract law doctrines ensure consent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How would you propose to distinguish between facially neutral contract law defenses that implicitly discriminate against arbitration and those that do not? What's the test you would use to tell the difference between the two?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gupta",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GUPTA: Obviously we concede that those kinds of rules are preempted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But what's your answer to his specific effort to assimilate the issue in this case, which is the class action, to the made-up issue, which you concede is a discrimination?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gupta",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GUPTA: Right. I think one of the purposes -- we agree with Petitioners about this. One of the purposes of the Federal Arbitration Act is to allow parties to contract their procedures, to tailor their procedures; and in general the courts ought not to be interfering with those kinds of consensual decisions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Can I take you back to a question that was asked a few minutes ago, because I'm not sure I understood your answer."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: No, but I really would appreciate it if you would answer my hypothetical on one that was posed before."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Where do we look to find the answer? I mean, I understand your answer and I know the other side's going to say: Well, this is a tremendous obstacle."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Is your test a purpose test or an effects test? Is it a test that says the State is doing this in order to kill arbitration, or is it a test that says the State is doing something that will kill arbitration?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Counsel",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the Tunkl case, which is a California Supreme Court case that we cite, the court makes clear that it's looking for contracts that -- in which public services are being performed and that are otherwise imbued with a public interest. It's not looking at all at the effects on third parties."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Counsel",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0003-0001",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are not aware as we say in our brief of any contract that explicitly permits class arbitrations for the reasons that the Court discussed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-893",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d330bae63973139a3d28594e6ff472e4eba3083f82204b76c5d45a363464bafd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d330bae63973-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
